Confirmed: `HelpCommand.execute` calls `command.getHelpText(prefix)`, and every command's cached `helpText` is overwritten by `reloadText()` immediately during server startup (`initialize()` → `startup()` → `loadResources()` → `commandParser.reload()`), before any player ever sees `/help`. This confirms the bug is live from server start, not just a theoretical edge case.

## Bug-Introducing Commits
BIC: c9fe5296dfa0c1c07a6a1d8a44a554c11485e804

## Reasoning

**(1) Investigation:** I traced the history of the files touched by the fix (`AbstractCommand.java`, `CommandParser.java`, `InvalidCommand.java`, `OnlinePlayerArgCommand.java`, `PlayerArgCommand.java`, `Server.java`) with `git log -p`. The fix's core change is removing a `helpText` caching mechanism (`reloadText()` triggered by `CommandParser.reload()`) and replacing it with an approach that computes the help text fresh on every call to `getHelpText()`, using a new immutable `help` field instead of the mutable `name` field. I then located the commit that first introduced this `reload()`/`reloadText()` caching mechanism: `c9fe5296dfa0c1c07a6a1d8a44a554c11485e804`. I checked every subsequent commit touching these files up to the fix (`0342a1b`, `68b1dd3`, `033cc96`, `e0453eb`, `ecdaf9e`, `c979eec`) and confirmed none of them altered the relevant logic — they only renamed color-code literals to `Color` constants or made unrelated generic/refactor changes.

**(2) The specific defect:** In `AbstractCommand`'s constructor (as introduced in `c9fe5296d`):
```java
protected AbstractCommand(String name, String commandCode) {
  if (name != null) {
    helpText = name + "\u00a7f : " + t.get(commandCode);   // uses FULL name, e.g. "ban PLAYER [REASON]"
    int splitIndex = name.indexOf(" ");
    if (splitIndex != -1) {
      name = name.substring(0, splitIndex);                // name is now truncated to "ban"
    }
  } ...
  this.name = name;   // truncated name stored as the field
}

public void reloadText() {
  if (name != null) {
    helpText = name + "\u00a7f : " + t.get(commandCode);    // BUG: reuses the already-truncated `name` field
  } ...
}
```
`reloadText()` rebuilds `helpText` using the `name` field, but by the time `reloadText()` can ever run, `name` has already been truncated (argument placeholders like `PLAYER [REASON]` stripped off) during construction. So every time `reload()` runs, the cached help text for any command with a parameterized usage string (e.g. `BanCommand`: `"ban PLAYER [REASON]"`) loses its argument hints and collapses to just the bare command name (`"ban"`).

**(3) Supporting evidence:** `CommandParser.reload()` is called unconditionally from `Server.loadResources()`, which itself runs during every server `startup()` (right after `initialize()` constructs all commands) and on every `/reload`. This means the corrupted help text is in effect from the very first moment the server is usable — `HelpCommand.execute()` (confirmed via `git show`) simply calls `command.getHelpText(prefix)`, which returns the corrupted cached string. The fix commit directly addresses this: it introduces a separate, never-mutated `help` field that preserves the original (untruncated) name string, and computes `getHelpText()` on demand rather than caching a value that gets clobbered by a stale/truncated `name`. This is a precise, mechanical match between the defect and the fix.

I'm highly confident in this identification — the root cause (reusing the truncated `name` instead of the original full usage string) is present unchanged from the moment the caching/reload mechanism was introduced in `c9fe5296d`, and no intervening commit altered that logic before the fix.

